\use{document}

\title {Balanced Columns}
\author {The texish project}
\date {June 2026}
\maketitle

\section {Three balanced columns}

The \texttt{columns} primitive sets its body as several balanced columns. The
text is typeset once at the column measure, then divided into equal-height pieces
with the same page-style breaking \texttt{vsplit} uses, and the pieces are stood
side by side. Balancing means the columns come out level, rather than filling the
first to the bottom before the next begins.

\columns{3}{\noindent
The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog, and then, gathering its breath, it
turns and jumps back again, because a single leap is never quite enough to settle
an old argument between a fox and a dog. Setting a paragraph in narrow columns
forces many short lines, which is exactly what makes column balancing visible:
the engine can choose a break between almost any pair of lines, so it can divide
the galley into three pieces of nearly equal height. When the text is poured into
the columns, each one fills to about a third of the total, and the reader's eye
travels down the first column, back up to the top of the second, and down again,
the way a newspaper or a two-column research paper is read.}

\vskip 18pt

\section {Two columns, wider gutter}

The same text in two columns, with a wider gutter set by the optional argument.

\columns gap:32 {2}{\noindent
The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog, and then, gathering its breath, it
turns and jumps back again, because a single leap is never quite enough to settle
an old argument between a fox and a dog. Two columns leave longer lines than
three, so the texture is closer to ordinary text while still balancing the
material into two level halves.}

\vfill\eject
